I think it's easier if you write it out in math notation.
The n isn't being bound in that summation, so I'm not sure
what it's indexing.  That summation has to sum over hk and pk.
In general:

  log p(a|b) = SUM_{c,d} p(a,c,d|b)

> My first question is: how may I include in bernoulli_log's parameters function when l'm interested (for Bernoulli) in the parameter sampled at the previous step by the uniform distribution? May you give me any suggestions for dealing with the marginalization in a loop?

I think it'll be clearer after looking at the manual chapter
examples.

I'm not sure what you mean by "parameters function".  You
can't literally do sampling in a Stan program block other than
for generated quantities (and that doesn't effect estimation).

>
> Then, if I assigned a normal distribution for the intercept beta0 ( for example ~ normal(0,0.01) ), may you think that the following Stan-statements are correct and optimal?
