From uda33143a8db60ce8 at groups.google.com  Fri Jan 23 03:17:25 2015
From: uda33143a8db60ce8 at groups.google.com (Andrew Gelman)
Date: Fri, 23 Jan 2015 03:17:25 +0000
Subject: comment on #warmup iterations needed
Message-ID: <CRMyv2ylD84.qhcNhCRtnmoJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <CRMyv2ylD84.dlVLe6zzrz4J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <CRMyv2ylD84.GJsCHq1eshEJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <CRMyv2ylD84.dlVLe6zzrz4J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/CRMyv2ylD84/m/qhcNhCRtnmoJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I recommend doing half the iterations for warmup and half for samples that you use.  Economizing on warmup isn’t always such an economy.  Or, to put it another way:  If you only need 50 warmup, I think you probably only need 50 for samples.  Or, if you want to be safe and have 350 for samples, I think you should really be safe and have 350 for warmup.

I have no proof on this (it’s an issue I’ve thought about, without success, for about 25 years) but that’s my impression.
A

P.S.  I’m glad the model runs beautifully!

